// @run
// @log s
// @log 1
// The ORDER-DEPENDENCE twin of `exported-generic-called.vl`. Same declaration, same two
// instantiations, call sites SWAPPED — and that swap changes the module's published ABI.
//
// An exported generic's export entry resolves by name to the FIRST `fnStmts` slot carrying
// it, which is instantiation 0. `exported-generic-called.vl` calls `id(1)` first and exports
// `(i32) -> i32`; this file calls `id("s")` first and exports `((ref 0)) -> ((ref 0))` —
// both measured on the export section.
//
// The export is TRUTHFUL either way: host-calling the exported `id` at its published type
// returns the identity, and the other instantiation is a separate private function that no
// importer can name. So this is a NARROWING, not a wrong answer — an importer cannot reach
// the string instantiation, and cannot call the i32 one wrongly either, because the wasm
// type system rejects the attempt. It is pinned here because the choice is made by SOURCE
// ORDER of in-module call sites, which is not a property anyone would expect their ABI to
// depend on, and a future change to instantiation ordering would flip it silently.
export function id<T>(x: T): T { return x }

print(id("s"))
print(id(1))
